From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 23:14:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 122/185] segacd: publish the driver's counters, and let the
 diagnostic be a program

The disc diagnostic was 222 lines inside biosmain. The only reason it
had to be there is that everything it prints is this driver's private
counters -- so those are published instead: a struct scd_api in the
cookie jar under 'SgCD', one call that fills a snapshot and one that
pulls the levers. That is how a TOS program has always found a driver.

What is left in the operating system is one Pexec of A:\CDTEST.PRG,
still on Down, because holding a button is the quickest way to start a
test on a console with no keyboard and because a program only the
desktop can launch is no use when the fault stops the desktop
appearing. The difference is that it is a program either way now: it
runs from the desktop whenever it is wanted, and it cannot take the
boot down with it -- which it did, the first time the drive-wake went
in front of it.

The image was four bytes under the wall where the CDBIOS parks. It is
2334 under it now, and 29 KB of RAM comes back with the 28 KB log
buffer, which the program owns instead.

One snapshot call rather than twenty accessors, so every number in a
printed round describes the same moment. Both structs carry their own
size and version, and a program that finds a size it was not built
against refuses to read the fields -- reading the wrong offsets
confidently is a thing this project has already done to itself.

The size check is not enough on its own, and finding that out cost a
build: control() first took `short` arguments. EmuTOS is compiled
-mshort and a .PRG is not, so one side pushed two bytes where the other
read four, every op arrived as op 0, and the struct was the same size
either way so nothing complained. long is four bytes to both.
